 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069220 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11047f98-24ac-5e85-994b-d2cd73e1984b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069220 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $25.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $25,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}+50y+25 & = \\\\ 25({y}^{2}+2y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y+1 & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot y+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({y}^{2}+2y+1) & = \\\\ 25(y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1061 4236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8854423e-52a6-5077-ab8f-c9de25d23206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069792 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1061 4237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac3a35ba-8b05-5a52-9f06-dc5add492619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069793 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1061 4242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a2dd26d-51b3-5cc1-8396-efeb5b1b8bb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069794 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1061 4244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3464306e-5c3f-55f3-9c10-87ab309dded2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069795 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069796 #edb/ref 1061 4245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3222e5cc-52d3-5afd-8468-ab3d87c483b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1061 5059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8accb58-b4d7-5dc8-9c5e-a5ca4f3588a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069876 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1061 6170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecc30896-0712-5c97-a35e-e51485979c95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, perfect square trinomials have a leading coefficient that is something other than $1.$ For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4x^2+12x+9\n$$\n\nhas a leading coefficient of $4,$ and it is a perfect square trinomial because it is the square of the binomial $(2x+3)$ as we can see below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+2(2x)(3)+(3)^{2} & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial with a leading coefficient other than $1,$ we use the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a+b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $4x^2+12x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+12x+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(6x)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $2x$ and ${\\color{blue}3}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+2(2x\\cdot 3)+(3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula. In this case, we have $a=2x$ and $b=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(2x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** This method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. But we will learn more general methods in the future." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1065 1597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5d43b5e8-672e-5cdd-b7a3-8de29e6552f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069940 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1064 "How can we factor a trinomial that has a negative leading coefficient, like\n\n$$\n-25y^2 + 20y - 4\\,?\n$$\n\n \nWe can reduce this to a more familiar problem by factoring out $−1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(25y^2 - 20y + 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}-20y+4 & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-20y+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(10y)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y)^{2}-2(5y\\cdot 2)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (5y-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(25{y}^{2}-20y+4) & = \\\\ -(5y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1065 1598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "757d1827-0402-5d3d-b8da-a4dc88c254bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069941 #edb/ref 1067 "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial With a Negative Leading Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1064 "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the square of a difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2} & =(a-b)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $9x^2-24x+16$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-24x+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Write the middle parentheses $(12x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(12x)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n4\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have $a=3x$ and $b=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2} & = \\\\ (3x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n**Note:** Once again, we note that this method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1065 1856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "609ce9b5-1b4e-5dfc-9d22-4ec6ea2d640b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069954 #edb/ref 1067 "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483580 #edb/ref 1080 "-(5x-4)^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483580 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483580 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06854b84-638b-5f5d-98ab-d1f9249c195b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483599 #edb/ref 1080 "25({y}^{2}+1)^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483599 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483599 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12aaa2df-cbfc-5d4a-8f1a-02ca44658d0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483611 #edb/ref 1080 "-(3x-2)^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483611 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483611 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a88e3be-86b1-5646-b222-bbff79557817" 