045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484190 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484190 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484190 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb1c333a-4bd7-5452-8866-0435e2c0c3f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484210 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484210 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484210 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484210 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $98{s}^{2}+84s+18.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484210 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "388f80cd-cabc-537e-b7cd-20f266912f80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484210 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $2.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98{s}^{2}+84s+18 & = \\\\ 2(49{s}^{2}+42s+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{s}^{2}+42s+9 & = \\\\ (7s)^{2}+42s+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (7s)^{2}+2\\cdot (7s\\cdot 3)+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (7s+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(49{s}^{2}+42s+9) & = \\\\ 2(7s+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484231 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484231 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44099" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484231 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484231 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor $-9{x}^{2}+12x-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66f132c4-a82c-5cf3-bd20-df4c38d0e1c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484231 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{x}^{2}+12x-4 & = \\\\ -(9{x}^{2}-12x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-12x+4 & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-12x+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2\\cdot (6x)+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (3x-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(9{x}^{2}-12x+4) & = \\\\ -(3x-2)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484249 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484249 #edb/ref 1071 "$25{x}^{2}-20x+4=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c53c9a4-fe1b-5044-8911-e4ffad0659c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484249 #edb/ref 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}=(a-b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n25{x}^{2}-20x+4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x)^{2}-20x+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x)^{2}-2(10x)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(10x)$ as a product of $5x$ and $2:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x)^{2}-2(5x\\cdot 2)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=5x$ and $b=2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484281 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484281 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484281 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484281 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor  $9{p}^{2}+30p+25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484281 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0c969ed-b329-51a2-928a-6ee83a4be196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484281 #edb/ref 1074 "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}=(a+b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9{p}^{2}+30p+25.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p)^{2}+30p+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p)^{2}+2(15p)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(15p)$ as a product of $3p$ and $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p)^{2}+2(3p\\cdot 5)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of a sum formula with $a=3p$ and $b=5.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484282 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484282 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484282 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484282 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor $-16{k}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}k-\\frac{1}{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484282 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1247d09-04db-57b2-b09e-dc5dafe32a21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484282 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{k}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}k-\\frac{1}{9} & = \\\\ -(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference  formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9} & = \\\\ (4k)^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ (4k)^{2}-2\\cdot (4k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9}) & = \\\\ -{(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484291 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484291 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484291 #edb/ref 1071 "$36{x}^{2}-12x+1=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1a4e854-18be-5044-a0a8-f098474cee63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484291 #edb/ref 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}=(a-b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n36{x}^{2}-12x+1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x)^{2}-12x+(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x)^{2}-2(6x)+(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $6x$ and $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x)^{2}-2(6x\\cdot 1)+(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=6x$ and $b=1.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484292 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor $-4{m}^{2}-4m-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2d099da-828c-5882-bf9e-a7fe2ace1301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484292 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{m}^{2}-4m-1 & = \\\\ -(4{m}^{2}+4m+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{m}^{2}+4m+1 & = \\\\ (2m)^{2}+4m+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (2m)^{2}+2\\cdot (2m\\cdot 1)+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (2m+1)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(4{m}^{2}+4m+1) & = \\\\ -(2m+1)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484295 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484295 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484295 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484295 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor $-4{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484295 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa78e3d6-5bc5-5702-a92b-18645aa6667d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484295 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25} & = \\\\ -(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2x)+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(2x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25}) & = \\\\ -{(2x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
